: false;
}
Not bad, right?
The alternative dynamic approach definitely looks more DRY and versatile than the
default one, all while retaining the same security level against SQL injection attacks.
The only downside may be due to the additional overhead brought by the
System.Linq.Dynamics.Core library, which will likely have some minor
performance impact. Although this shouldn't be an issue in most scenarios, whenever
we want our APIs to respond to HTTP requests as quickly as possible, we should
arguably favor the default approach.
